() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 5.1 Active to Passive Voice in Scientific Communication) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(Voice indicates whether the grammatical subject performs the action \(Active\) or receives) Tj
T*
(the action \(Passive\).) Tj
T*
(* Structural Mechanism:) Tj
T*
(Active: Subject + Verb + Direct Object.) Tj
T*
(Passive: Direct Object becomes Subject + Appropriate Tense Form of 'be' + Past) Tj
T*
(Participle \(V3\) + [by Agent].) Tj
T*
(* Tense-Specific Passive Paradigms:) Tj
T*
(- Simple Present: is/are + V3 \("The enzyme is purified"\).) Tj
T*
(- Simple Past: was/were + V3 \("The flask was autoclaved"\).) Tj
T*
(- Present Continuous: is/are being + V3 \("The sample is being analyzed"\).) Tj
T*
(- Present Perfect: has/have been + V3 \("The culture has been inoculated"\).) Tj
T*
(- Modals: modal + be + V3 \("The mixture must be heated"\).) Tj
T*
(* Stylistic Rationale in Technical Reports: Scientific prose prioritizes the) Tj
T*
(experimental phenomenon, methodology, and outcome over the human experimenter.) Tj
T*
(Passive voice creates an objective, impersonal, and reproducible tone: "The) Tj
T*
(precipitate was washed three times" is vastly preferred over "I washed the) Tj
T*
(precipitate three times.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 5.2 Modal Auxiliaries and Degrees of Epistemic Certainty) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(Modals express probability, ability, necessity, obligation, and scientific hedging.) Tj
T*
(* Epistemic Modals \(Degrees of Scientific Probability\):) Tj
T*
(- Will / Must: Near-certainty / Deduced factual inevitability \(>95% certainty\):) Tj
T*
("The protein must have denatured at 90 deg C.") Tj
T*
(- Should / Ought to: High expectation / Normal operating probability \(~70-80%\):) Tj
T*
("The reaction should reach completion in 30 minutes.") Tj
T*
(- May: Moderate possibility \(~50%\): "The mutation may alter ligand binding) Tj
T*
(affinity.") Tj
T*
(- Could / Might: Weak possibility / Scientific speculation \(<30%\): "The observed) Tj
T*
(anomaly might be attributed to minor pipetting error.") Tj
T*
(* Deontic Modals \(Obligation and Rules\):) Tj
T*
(- Must: Strong internal or mandatory safety obligation: "Personnel must wear) Tj
T*
(nitrile gloves.") Tj
T*
(- Have to: External institutional rule or regulatory mandate: "The laboratory has) Tj
T*
(to comply with OSHA biohazard guidelines.") Tj
T*
(- Should: Moral recommendation or best laboratory practice: "Buffers should be) Tj
T*
(prepared freshly.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

endstream
